# PDF Reading

Always use the view_file tool to read academic pdfs. Avoid using any python lib as the first option.

# File Encoding

All file operations (creation, opening, reading, writing, and editing) must strictly use UTF-8 encoding.
- Never use ASCII or system-default legacy encodings (such as Windows-1252/CP-1252).
- The project strictly works with Spanish text, which requires full support for accented characters (á, é, í, ó, ú), ñ, diéresis (ü), and inverted punctuation (¿, ¡).
- When writing Python code or scripts, always explicitly specify `encoding='utf-8'` when reading or writing files (e.g., `open(file, 'r', encoding='utf-8')`).
- Shell and command-line operations must preserve and emit UTF-8 encoding.

# Python Environment & Testing (Windows / PowerShell)

- DO NOT invoke bare `pytest` in PowerShell/CMD (it is not in the system PATH and causes CommandNotFoundException).
- DO NOT invoke bare `python -m pytest` directly (the Windows global Python lacks pytest and workspace dependencies).
- ALWAYS use one of the following canonical commands to execute tests:
  1. Virtual environment runner directly:
     `.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest <args>`
  2. Workspace test runner (auto-resolves venv):
     `python run_tests.py <args>`
  3. Direct venv pytest binary:
     `.\.venv\Scripts\pytest.exe <args>`
- For individual scripts or modules, always invoke using the virtual environment:
  `.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m src.validator.cli <args>`

